  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in Illinois through FY2026 to date shows $9.5B in obligations, distributed across 409,480 awards with an average award size of $23.3K. Obligations fell 22.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $4.8B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $1.8B and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $1.7B. Leading vendors include UCHICAGO ARGONNE, LLC and W S DARLEY & CO at $1B apiece, and MEDLINE INDUSTRIES, LP at $860M. Major industry categories include Medical, Dental, and Hospital Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers at $1.1B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1B. Recent fiscal-year comparisons show FY21 at $11.9B, FY22 at $13.6B, FY23 at $13B, FY24 at $13.4B, FY25 at $12.4B, and FY26 at $9.5B. This snapshot covers partial current-year totals, reported as contract obligations for the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting changed in Illinois year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 22.9% year over year. Fiscal-year totals progressed from $11.9B in FY21 to $13.6B in FY22, $13B in FY23, $13.4B in FY24, $12.4B in FY25, and $9.5B in FY26. The drop to $9.5B in FY26 coincides with the current-year totals being partial and reported as fiscal year to date. Average award size stands at $23.3K across 409,480 awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and suppliers in Illinois federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads buyers with $4.8B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy at $1.8B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $1.7B. Top vendors include UCHICAGO ARGONNE, LLC and W S DARLEY & CO at $1B each, and MEDLINE INDUSTRIES, LP at $860M. The top industry categories are Medical, Dental, and Hospital Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers at $1.1B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1B."
      }
    ]
